  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Across Anthropology shows clear early uptake as a scholarly and teaching-oriented book: it has been cited 3 times, downloaded 10,212 times through OAPEN, and appears in a recorded discussion tied to museum and curatorial practice. The record also shows institutional support from KU Leuven, Humboldt-Universit\u00e4t zu Berlin, and CARMAH, suggesting the book has circulated across research and pedagogical contexts.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Across Anthropology is already moving beyond a narrowly disciplinary audience. The strongest evidence is usage: more than 10,000 OAPEN downloads indicate substantial readership, while the book\u2019s presence in open repositories and library holdings points to broad discoverability. Its hybrid open-access status likely helps this reach by making the work available through multiple channels rather than a single access point.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Citation evidence is still modest but meaningful. OpenAlex records 3 citations, all in journal articles, showing that the book is beginning to be taken up in scholarly argument. The citing venues span Performance Research, International Journal of Cultural Policy, and Intersections, which suggests that the book\u2019s themes travel across anthropology, performance, and cultural-policy conversations rather than remaining confined to one subfield.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The topical profile of the citing literature also reflects the book\u2019s interdisciplinary relevance. The cited work is being used in discussions of restitution, knowledge production, postcolonial transition, memory, and performance, aligning with the book\u2019s own concern with anthropology \u201celsewhere\u201d and \u201cotherwise,\u201d as well as its attention to museums, curatorial practice, and colonial legacies. That pattern supports an interpretation of the book as a conceptual bridge between anthropology and adjacent humanities and social-science debates.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Teaching and public-facing use are also visible. A recorded YouTube discussion from Staatliche Kunstsammlungen Dresden indicates that the book has been used in an interpretive or educational setting focused on colonial legacies, museums, and the curatorial. Combined with library subject classifications in anthropology, museum techniques, postcolonialism, and anthropological ethics, this points to the book functioning as a pedagogical anchor for readers working on collections, decolonial critique, and exhibition-making.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the record depicts a book with strong circulation, early scholarly citation, and credible teaching adoption. The evidence is not yet extensive in citation terms, but it is consistent with a work that has found attention in both academic and practice-oriented communities around anthropology, museums, and postcolonial inquiry.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
